##

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.020 line:91%
This video is sponsored by
CuriosityStream

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.540 line:85%
and my own Nebula original series.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.860 line:85%
More on that later.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.340 line:91%
In 2019, a load of music videos
that had been uploaded to YouTube

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.100 line:91%
in the early days of the platform
were remastered.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.380 line:91%
YouTube let the music labels keep
the same video IDs,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.800 position:59% line:85%
along with all the views and comments,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.360 line:91%
so suddenly,
all those old videos were in HD!

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.480 line:85%
And they look...

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.160 line:85%
well, it depends on the video.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.240 line:64%
Smash Mouth's <i>All Star</i> looks awful.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.100 line:91%
Now, there are some brilliant
remasters out there.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.160 position:56% line:91%
Wham!'s <i>Last Christmas</i> is now in 4K,
and it looks spectacular.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.780 position:61% line:91%
And that's because it was shot on film,
full cinema quality,

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.060 position:67% line:91%
the sort of thing you can literally project
on the side of a building

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.540 line:85%
and it will look great.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.260 position:70% line:91%
Same with Freddie Mercury's <i>Living On My Own</i>,
the remaster looks stunning.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.800 line:85%
In fact, I think that looks better,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.380 line:91%
because they haven't converted it
to widescreen:

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.260 position:58% line:91%
they've kept it at the "aspect ratio"
of old '80s television.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.260 line:91%
Because music videos
weren't meant for the cinema.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.780 line:91%
They were meant for television.
It's in the name: music video.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.620 position:56% line:85%
So one of the first steps in editing

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.360 line:91%
will have been putting the film
through a telecine,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.420 position:63% line:91%
which is a very fancy way of
pointing a TV camera at a film projector

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.220 position:56% line:85%
and saving the results to videotape.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.020 position:63% line:91%
It is way more precise than your average
movie-theatre pirate with a camcorder,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:13.320 position:69% line:91%
but the result is that all that film quality
gets crushed down

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.960 position:58% line:91%
onto a standard definition TV picture
stored on magnetic tape.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.520
480 horizontal lines in the US,
576 in Europe. That's it.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.860 line:85%
Any more quality than that is gone.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.300 line:91%
Editing on videotape is
much cheaper and easier, though,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.660 position:61% line:91%
and if you're putting the final version
out on television, well...

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.120 line:91%
why would you bother with
editing actual physical film,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.840 line:91%
which is what you had to do
back then?

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.680 position:70% line:91%
A few videos might have gone to that expense,
if it was part of a big concert film, say.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.940 position:67% line:91%
So the <i>Rolling Stones Rock and Roll Circus </i>
was played at the New York Film Festival,

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.140
and you can see the cinema camera
with its big, bulky rolls of film

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.200
in the background of
some of the shots.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.860 position:58%
But the final version of
most music videos were, well, videos.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.540 position:59% line:92%
So for the remaster of <i>Last Christmas,</i>

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:59.100 position:58% line:91%
a team of engineers had to track down
the original reels of film,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.920 line:91%
and rescan them using
modern technology at 4K,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.560 line:91%
and then, they painstakingly took
those 4K files

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.620 line:91%
and recreated every edit
in the original video,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.100 line:91%
frame for frame,
cut for cut.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.620 position:72%
Actually, they were only able to find
seven out of the eight original reels of film,

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.580 line:92%
so there are a few shots in there

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.280
where the only source material was
the TV-quality music video.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.500 line:85%
And you can tell,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.940 position:86% line:91%
'cause this is what an old-school, standard-definition,
analogue signal looks like

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:28.240 position:56% line:91%
when you use world-class technicians
to upscale it to 4K.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.780 line:85%
It's not too bad? I guess?

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.500 line:91%
<i>Living On My Own</i>
also has a couple of shots

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.980 position:59% line:68%
where the old '80s digital effects
have to be recreated with modern tech,

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.960 line:64%
and... they're not a perfect match.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.460 position:56% line:68%
And there are also a couple of shots
that have been replaced,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.780 line:68%
or the timing slightly changed,
no idea why.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.020 line:64%
But again, it's not a bad job.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.480 line:68%
Now, there is a cheaper
remastering option

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.340 line:68%
that doesn't involve recreating
the whole edit from scratch:

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.160 line:85%
you can take the final TV edit

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.140 position:67% line:91%
from the best, cleanest, professional-grade
videotape you can find,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.040 line:85%
and upscale that to HD.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.800 line:91%
A lot of remastered videos
have done this.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.060 line:91%
It won't be as sharp as
the original film,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.380 line:91%
but it will look better than
people are used to.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.700 position:69% line:91%
Which is what they've done with
the remastered version of <i>Bohemian Rhapsody.</i>

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:12.520 line:85%
And look,

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.720 position:64% line:91%
I can see why record companies would want
an HD version of <i>Bohemian Rhapsody.</i>

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.280 position:61% line:91%
It's iconic.
It has over a billion views on YouTube.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.740 line:85%
But this was not shot on film.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.640 line:91%
It was recorded on TV cameras,
edited on videotape—

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.100 line:91%
you can tell by
the look of the lighting—

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.960 position:67% line:91%
and by the fact that these effects required
live feedback to the camera.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.980 position:66% line:91%
you could only get this by pointing
a television camera directly at a monitor,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.180 line:91%
which means there was never
any high-quality film version,

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.300 position:66% line:85%
this was only ever in standard definition.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.700 position:69% line:91%
And remember, old-school analogue television
in Europe runs at 50 frames a second.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.920 line:91%
60 in the US,
but 50 in Europe.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.760 position:63% line:91%
Half of the horizontal lines get skipped
each time, which is called interlacing.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.720 position:56% line:91%
And it means you get less flickering
on old equipment.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.840 position:78% line:91%
So if you want the authentic, original experience,
as seen back in the '70s,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.000 position:61% line:91%
then <i>Bohemian Rhapsody</i> should be played
at standard definition,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.760 line:85%
at 50 interlaced frames a second.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.780 line:85%
That's what was recorded.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.280 line:91%
Instead, what you see on YouTube is
a computer's best effort

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.560 position:70% line:91%
to make a high-definition, 25 frame a second,
film-like picture from that limited data,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.960 position:61% line:91%
filling in the gaps,
turning jagged edges into smooth lines.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:23.760 line:91%
That's called 'deinterlacing'
and it's not done badly here.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.120 position:64% line:91%
'Remastering' doesn't mean 'restoration'.
It means 'recreation'.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:32.380 position:75% line:68%
You'll see marketing blurbs saying
remasters are "the way it was meant to be seen".

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.000 line:64%
Which is a great tagline, but...

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.060 line:64%
no, it wasn't.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.900 line:68%
Remastering is, to an extent,
changing the historical record.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.340 line:68%
There are fan remasters of
Bohemian Rhapsody out there,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.880 position:63% line:68%
upscaled from VHS, 50 frames a second,
that are much more true to the original.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.960 line:91%
But at least Bohemian Rhapsody got
a decent HD remaster.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.200 line:91%
It's not like what they've done
to Smash Mouth.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.420 line:85%
Look at his face in this scene.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.800 position:67% line:91%
That's not a clear high-definition picture,
that's some kind of demon child!

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.140 line:91%
It's scaled up wrongly,
it's over-sharpened.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:07.080 line:91%
Look at the diagonal lines here,
they're jagged and stepped.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.420 position:69% line:91%
I tracked down a standard-definition version
from years ago that someone had reuploaded,

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:15.020 line:91%
and... yeah, I can see
why a computer would do that.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.460 position:58% line:91%
And, oh yeah, it's badly deinterlaced
in some parts too,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:23.040 position:69% line:91%
because here, you can see the cheerleader is
in two places at once in the same frame.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.760 line:91%
It's not bleed-over
from the next frame either.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.820 position:69% line:85%
She's in two different places there as well.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.280 position:56% line:85%
Same for this scene with the dancer.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.200 position:61% line:91%
Why is there a dancer in just one scene
in this music video?

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.200 position:70% line:91%
But those deinterlacing artifacts,
I don't understand how they're even possible.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.420 position:56% line:91%
Did they shoot some of this
on a TV camera at 60 frames a second

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.320 position:56% line:85%
and then grade it to look like film?

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.620 line:85%
That doesn't make any sense,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.220 line:91%
but that's the only way
that those artifacts could happen.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.760 position:63% line:91%
But other parts look fine, and look like
they're on film at 30 frames a second!

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.780 position:77% line:91%
And the parts that they pulled from
the movie that it was a tie-in to — <i>Mystery Men</i> —

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.040 line:85%
those run at 24 frames a second,

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.500 position:78% line:68%
and you can see in this transition, the right side
is badly deinterlaced with a ghost image

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.440 position:73% line:68%
and the left side has every fifth frame doubled
so the frame rates match.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:03.440 line:64%
I— I—

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.000 line:91%
I genuinely cannot understand
what has happened here.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.520 line:85%
It is a complete and utter mess.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.100 position:63% line:91%
Presumably some underpaid and overworked
VFX tech was given

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.840 line:91%
a low-quality, already-broken
copy of it and told:

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.940 position:59% line:91%
"It's just Smash Mouth, we don't care.
Just make it work."

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.600 line:91%
So they threw the video into a
basic resizer to make it HD,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.920 line:85%
massively dialed up the sharpness,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.280 position:58% line:91%
and tried to squeeze the aspect ratio
into... whatever this is.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:31.860 line:91%
The HD remaster looks worse
than the one it replaced.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.260 line:91%
It's sharper, sure,
but it's not better.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.580 line:91%
And if you are going to change
the historical record,

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.340 line:91%
it does seem a terrible option
to make it worse.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.620 position:67% line:91%
I've got an original series over on Nebula.
Here's the trailer.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.560 position:64% line:85%
<i>I invited five people to play some games.</i>

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.580 align:start size:94% position:6% line:91%
<c.yellow>I trust no one.</c>
<c.cyan>None of us are trustworthy.</c>

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.600 line:91%
<i>...in an environment designed to
slowly break their team apart.</i>

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.140 line:85%
<c.yellow>This is real money!</c>

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.660 line:91%
<i>But all they knew is
they'd be sat around a table</i>

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.880 line:85%
<i>trying to win real cash: $10,000.</i>

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.020 position:56% line:85%
<c.yellow>The vibe's changed after that theft.</c>

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.440 line:91%
<i>This is a show about trust,
about loyalty,</i>

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.380 line:85%
<i>and about Money.</i>

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.540 align:start size:84% position:16% line:91%
<c.yellow><i>Tom wants the chaos.</i></c>
<i>(all laugh)</i>

